--synopsis  
Eli Mercer ponders the meanings and origins of words, along with their winding evolution through time and usage.
--synopsis  
  
more_label: Lyrics    
    
--more  
[Verse 1]  
The Romans had a word they'd drop  
Before the thing they knew you knew —  
Scilicet — "it goes without saying"  
So of course they'd say it too.  
Two thousand years, the letters slurred,  
The schoolboys wore their license out —  
I'll take the c and let it go,  
And spell it like I hear it in my mouth.  
[Pre-Chorus]  
[cello enters low beneath the last line]  
They said we couldn't mean things now,  
Said the wound of irony was too wide —  
[Chorus]  
Silaset, silaset —  
The obvious, said anyway.  
Silaset, silaset —  
I love you. This matters. Stay.  
[Verse 2]  
I've been through every doubt they stacked,  
Clocked the posture of the knowing smirk.  
But the joke wore thin and the meaning didn't —  
It just kept showing up for work.  
So I'll quest after meaning,  
I'll laugh at my own immateriality,  
Stand here stupid as a lit lamp  
In a room that's already bright.  
[Chorus]  
Silaset, silaset —  
The obvious, said anyway.  
Silaset, silaset —  
I love you. This matters. Stay.  
[Bridge]  
[strings swell, guitar drops to sparse]  
Take the k out of knight,  
Take the b out of doubt,  
Take the c out of scilicet,  
Let the sound carry out.  
The spelling was a chain we wore,  
The silent letters a kind of shame —  
Say it like you mean it now,  
Give the obvious a name.  
[Final Chorus]  
[full arrangement, still unhurried]  
Silaset, silaset —  
Even knowing what we know.  
Silaset, silaset —  
Here's the thing we always knew.  
Silaset, silaset —  
I love you. This matters. Stay.  
[Outro]  
[voice alone, guitar returns soft, cello holds a drone]  
Silaset.  
Silaset.  
(It goes without saying —)  
Silaset.  
[Fade Out]  
--more    
